---

<BaseLayout
  title="JSON Schema"
  description="Measured Draft-07 conformance (828/929 = 89.13%), the two front doors into Luq from a schema, the full keyword mapping, and every keyword that is not supported."
>
  <Header />
  <MobileMenuOverlay navItems={navItems} />

  <main class="mx-auto w-full max-w-5xl px-4 py-12 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 lg:py-16">
    <!-- ================= Hero ================= -->
    <header class="mb-12">
      <p class="mb-2 text-label-large text-primary">Draft-07</p>
      <h1 class="mb-4 text-display-small text-on-surface">JSON Schema</h1>
      <p class="max-w-3xl text-body-large text-on-surface-variant">
        Luq validates a Draft-07 document by <strong class="text-on-surface">converting</strong> it
        into the same field declarations you would have written by hand. There is no second engine:
        the plan a schema produces is the plan <code>.v()</code> produces, so a converted document
        composes with everything else in the library.
      </p>
    </header>

    <!-- ================= Conformance ================= -->
    <section id="conformance" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
      <h2 class="mb-2 text-headline-medium text-on-surface">Conformance, measured</h2>
      <p class="mb-6 max-w-3xl text-body-medium text-on-surface-variant">
        Against the official JSON-Schema-Test-Suite draft7 corpus, required tests only. Skipped
        cases are counted as <strong class="text-on-surface">failures</strong>, and the skip list is
        asserted to be exactly the failing set in both directions — so a case cannot be hidden to
        raise the number, and a skipped case that starts passing fails the build too.
      </p>

      <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-3">
        <div class="md-card md-card-filled p-6">
          <p class="text-label-medium text-on-surface-variant">This release</p>
          <p class="mt-1 text-display-small text-primary">{conformance.percent}%</p>
          <p class="mt-1 text-body-small text-on-surface-variant">
            {conformance.passed} of {conformance.total} cases
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="md-card md-card-outlined p-6">
          <p class="text-label-medium text-on-surface-variant">A validator that always says yes</p>
          <p class="mt-1 text-display-small text-on-surface-variant">
            {conformance.trivialPercent}%
          </p>
          <p class="mt-1 text-body-small text-on-surface-variant">
            {conformance.trivialPassed} of {conformance.total} — the floor the number above should
            be read against
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="md-card md-card-outlined p-6">
          <p class="text-label-medium text-on-surface-variant">Luq 1.x, same corpus</p>
          <p class="mt-1 text-display-small text-on-surface-variant">
            {conformance.legacyPercent}%
          </p>
          <p class="mt-1 text-body-small text-on-surface-variant">
            {conformance.legacyPassed} of {conformance.total} — below the always-yes floor
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-6 md-card md-card-outlined overflow-hidden">
        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="w-full min-w-[40rem] border-collapse text-body-medium">
            <caption class="px-4 pt-4 text-left text-title-small text-on-surface">
              Where the score comes from
            </caption>
            <thead>
              <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant text-label-large text-on-surface-variant">
                <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-medium"></th>
                <th class="px-4 py-3 text-right font-medium">
                  should be accepted ({conformance.validCases})
                </th>
                <th class="px-4 py-3 text-right font-medium">
                  should be rejected ({conformance.invalidCases})
                </th>
                <th class="px-4 py-3 text-right font-medium">total</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/60">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-on-surface-variant">Always says yes</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-right font-mono text-on-surface-variant">551 (100%)</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-right font-mono text-on-surface-variant">0 (0%)</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-right font-mono text-on-surface-variant">
                  {conformance.trivialPassed} ({conformance.trivialPercent}%)
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/60">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-on-surface-variant">Luq 1.x</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-right font-mono text-on-surface-variant">512 (92.92%)</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-right font-mono text-on-surface-variant">
                  {conformance.legacyInvalidPassed} ({conformance.legacyInvalidPercent}%)
                </td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-right font-mono text-on-surface-variant">
                  {conformance.legacyPassed} ({conformance.legacyPercent}%)
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-primary/[0.06]">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-on-surface">This release</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-right font-mono text-on-surface">
                  {conformance.validPassed} ({conformance.validPercent}%)
                </td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-right font-mono text-on-surface">
                  {conformance.invalidPassed} ({conformance.invalidPercent}%)
                </td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-right font-mono text-on-surface">
                  {conformance.passed} ({conformance.percent}%)
                </td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="border-t border-outline-variant px-4 py-3 text-body-small text-on-surface-variant">
          The column that moved is the second one. 1.x rejected 24 of the 378 documents it was
          supposed to reject; “JSON Schema support” was, on this corpus, letting almost everything
          through. This release loses 4 cases on the accepted side, because it refuses an external
          <code>$ref</code> at build time instead of quietly passing it.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-6 grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="md-card md-card-filled p-6">
          <h3 class="mb-2 text-title-medium text-on-surface">Excluding external <code>$ref</code></h3>
          <p class="text-body-medium text-on-surface-variant">
            57 of the 101 failures are cases where the suite serves a schema over HTTP and expects
            the validator to fetch it. Luq ships no network loader. Against the remaining
            {conformance.withoutExternalRefTotal} cases the score is
            <strong class="text-on-surface"
              >{conformance.withoutExternalRefPassed} / {conformance.withoutExternalRefTotal} = {
                conformance.withoutExternalRefPercent
              }%.</strong
            > Both numbers are true; the headline figure is the stricter one.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="md-card md-card-filled p-6">
          <h3 class="mb-2 text-title-medium text-on-surface">Which door was measured</h3>
          <p class="text-body-medium text-on-surface-variant">
            The corpus was run through the <strong class="text-on-surface">chain method</strong>,
            because its schemas put constraints on the document root and only that route can treat
            the root as the field itself. On the 289 cases whose instance is a plain object, the two
            doors score 238 (82.35%) for <code>.jsonSchemaFullFeature()</code> and 229 (79.24%) for
            <code>fromJsonSchema</code>, which is a <code>Validator&lt;T extends object&gt;</code>
            and so only validates objects.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-6 md-card md-card-outlined p-6">
        <h3 class="mb-3 text-title-medium text-on-surface">The corpus is pinned</h3>
        <dl class="grid gap-x-8 gap-y-2 text-body-medium sm:grid-cols-2">
          <div class="flex justify-between gap-4">
            <dt class="text-on-surface-variant">Submodule</dt>
            <dd class="font-mono text-on-surface">{corpus.submodule}</dd>
          </div>
          <div class="flex justify-between gap-4">
            <dt class="text-on-surface-variant">Commit</dt>
            <dd class="font-mono text-on-surface">{corpus.commit.slice(0, 10)}</dd>
          </div>
          <div class="flex justify-between gap-4">
            <dt class="text-on-surface-variant">Directory</dt>
            <dd class="font-mono text-on-surface">{corpus.directory}</dd>
          </div>
          <div class="flex justify-between gap-4">
            <dt class="text-on-surface-variant">Files / groups / cases</dt>
            <dd class="font-mono text-on-surface">
              {corpus.files} / {corpus.groups} / {corpus.cases}
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div class="flex justify-between gap-4 sm:col-span-2">
            <dt class="text-on-surface-variant">Corpus sha256</dt>
            <dd class="break-all text-right font-mono text-body-small text-on-surface">
              {corpus.sha256}
            </dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
        <p class="mt-4 text-body-small text-on-surface-variant">
          Move the submodule and the hash changes, and the build fails until the score is
          re-measured. A change in conformance is therefore always a reviewable commit. If the
          corpus is not checked out, the suite does not go green — one test fails saying so, so a
          number can never appear without a measurement behind it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ================= Two front doors ================= -->
    <section id="two-doors" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
      <h2 class="mb-2 text-headline-medium text-on-surface">Two front doors</h2>
      <p class="mb-8 max-w-3xl text-body-medium text-on-surface-variant">
        There is no <code>.fromJsonSchema()</code> builder method — the rewrite has no
        builder-extension mechanism at all. What exists is a <strong class="text-on-surface">function</strong>
        that converts a whole document, and a <strong class="text-on-surface">chain method</strong>
        that constrains one declared field by a document.
      </p>

      <div class="mb-8">
        <div class="mb-3 flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-3">
          <h3 class="text-title-large text-on-surface">1. <code>fromJsonSchema</code> — a whole document</h3>
          <span class="inline-flex h-7 items-center rounded-shape-full bg-tertiary-container px-3 text-label-medium text-on-tertiary-container">
            start here
          </span>
        </div>
        <p class="mb-4 max-w-3xl text-body-medium text-on-surface-variant">
          One import. Returns the same <code>Validator&lt;T&gt;</code> the builder returns:
          <code>validate</code>, <code>parse</code>, <code>pick</code>, <code>pickAll</code>,
          results shaped the same way, issue paths in the same <code>lines[0].sku</code> grammar.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock code={fullFeatureExample} fileName="account-validator.ts" />
      </div>

      <div class="mb-8">
        <h3 class="mb-3 text-title-large text-on-surface">
          2. <code>.jsonSchema()</code> — one declared field
        </h3>
        <p class="mb-4 max-w-3xl text-body-medium text-on-surface-variant">
          <code>@maroonedog/luq/plugins/jsonSchema</code> adds a chain method, and it takes the
          plugin bag explicitly. This method also reaches keywords the document
          <strong class="text-on-surface"> root</strong> cannot otherwise use: root-level
          <code>additionalProperties</code>, <code>anyOf</code>, <code>not</code>,
          <code>if</code>/<code>then</code>/<code>else</code>, <code>minProperties</code> and
          <code>propertyNames</code> are reachable this way and no other, because
          <code>fromJsonSchema</code> declares rules per field and the empty path is not a field.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock code={chainMethodExample} fileName="envelope-validator.ts" />
      </div>

      <div class="mb-8">
        <h3 class="mb-3 text-title-large text-on-surface">
          The bundled plugin has its own method name
        </h3>
        <p class="mb-4 max-w-3xl text-body-medium text-on-surface-variant">
          <code>jsonSchemaFullFeaturePlugin</code> is the same implementation with the 49-plugin bag
          already inside it, so its chain method takes no bag — and it is called
          <code>.jsonSchemaFullFeature()</code>, not <code>.jsonSchema()</code>. Two plugins may not
          claim one method on one slot; 1.x let both bundles claim <code>.fromJsonSchema</code> and
          picked a winner by registration order.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock code={bundledChainExample} fileName="reading-validator.ts" />
      </div>

      <div>
        <h3 class="mb-3 text-title-large text-on-surface">A document you only know at run time</h3>
        <p class="mb-4 max-w-3xl text-body-medium text-on-surface-variant">
          This works under a Content-Security-Policy that forbids <code>unsafe-eval</code>: the
          conversion builds closures, never source text. There is no <code>eval</code> and no
          <code>new Function</code> anywhere in the 762 emitted modules, and that is checked
          mechanically rather than asserted.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock code={runtimeSchemaExample} fileName="load-validator.ts" />
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ================= Which one ================= -->
    <section id="which-door" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
      <h2 class="mb-2 text-headline-medium text-on-surface">Which one to use</h2>
      <p class="mb-6 max-w-3xl text-body-medium text-on-surface-variant">
        <code>./plugins/jsonSchema</code> is billed as the tree-shakeable half. Measured with the
        same esbuild and gzip method as the rest of the size table, it is not — supplying a working
        bag costs 12 bytes <em>less</em> than importing the bundle, which is 0.06%.
      </p>

      <div class="md-card md-card-outlined overflow-hidden">
        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="w-full min-w-[38rem] border-collapse text-body-medium">
            <thead>
              <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant text-label-large text-on-surface-variant">
                <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-medium">Entry</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-3 text-right font-medium">gzip</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/60">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-mono text-body-small text-on-surface-variant">
                  Builder + jsonSchemaPlugin, no bag
                </td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-right font-mono text-on-surface-variant">18,992 B</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/60">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-mono text-body-small text-on-surface">
                  Builder + jsonSchemaPlugin + a working 49-plugin bag
                </td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-right font-mono text-on-surface">21,371 B</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-mono text-body-small text-on-surface">
                  Builder + jsonSchemaFullFeaturePlugin
                </td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-right font-mono text-on-surface">21,383 B</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-body-medium text-on-surface-variant">
        The first row is not a usable configuration: <code>.jsonSchema()</code> needs a bag. So use
        <code>jsonSchemaFullFeature</code>, and reach for <code>./plugins/jsonSchema</code> when you
        want the per-field chain method — not to save bytes.
        <a class="text-primary underline underline-offset-2" href="/benchmarks#json-schema-size">
          The method behind these figures is on the benchmarks page.
        </a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ================= Keyword mapping ================= -->
    <section id="keywords" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
      <h2 class="mb-2 text-headline-medium text-on-surface">Every Draft-07 keyword</h2>
      <p class="mb-6 max-w-3xl text-body-medium text-on-surface-variant">
        All {keywordTotals.total} of them — the count is checked against the draft-07 metaschema's
        own <code>properties</code> keys by a test. Each is one of three decisions, written down,
        with no fall-through: 1.x stored eleven keywords it then never read again.
      </p>

      <div class="mb-8 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-3">
        <div class="md-card md-card-filled p-5">
          <p class="text-headline-small text-on-surface">{keywordTotals.bind}</p>
          <p class="mt-1 text-label-large text-on-surface">bind</p>
          <p class="mt-1 text-body-small text-on-surface-variant">
            Straight to a chain method, with the method name checked by the compiler.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="md-card md-card-filled p-5">
          <p class="text-headline-small text-on-surface">{keywordTotals.structural}</p>
          <p class="mt-1 text-label-large text-on-surface">structural</p>
          <p class="mt-1 text-body-small text-on-surface-variant">
            The converter expands it — it recurses, or it picks the slot. A sub-schema becomes a
            sub-chain, which no binding can carry.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="md-card md-card-filled p-5">
          <p class="text-headline-small text-on-surface">{keywordTotals.annotation}</p>
          <p class="mt-1 text-label-large text-on-surface">annotation only</p>
          <p class="mt-1 text-body-small text-on-surface-variant">
            No validation effect in Draft-07, so nothing is generated. Each one says why in the
            source.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      {
        keywordGroups.map((group) => (
          <div class="mb-8">
            <h3 class="mb-1 text-title-large text-on-surface">{group.title}</h3>
            <p class="mb-3 max-w-3xl text-body-medium text-on-surface-variant">{group.blurb}</p>
            <div class="md-card md-card-outlined overflow-hidden">
              <div class="overflow-x-auto">
                <table class="w-full min-w-[48rem] border-collapse text-body-medium">
                  <thead>
                    <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant text-label-large text-on-surface-variant">
                      <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-medium">Keyword</th>
                      <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-medium">Handling</th>
                      <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-medium">Plugin and method</th>
                      <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-medium">Note</th>
                    </tr>
                  </thead>
                  <tbody>
                    {group.rows.map((row) => (
                      <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/60 last:border-0">
                        <td class="whitespace-nowrap px-4 py-3 align-top font-mono text-body-small text-on-surface">
                          {row.keyword}
                        </td>
                        <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top">
                          <span
                            class:list={[
                              'inline-flex h-7 items-center whitespace-nowrap rounded-shape-full px-3 text-label-medium',
                              handlingStyles[row.handling],
                            ]}
                          >
                            {handlingLabels[row.handling]}
                          </span>
                        </td>
                        <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top font-mono text-body-small text-on-surface-variant">
                          {row.target}
                        </td>
                        <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-body-small text-on-surface-variant">
                          {row.note}
                        </td>
                      </tr>
                    ))}
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </section>

    <!-- ================= Formats ================= -->
    <section id="formats" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
      <h2 class="mb-2 text-headline-medium text-on-surface">All twenty <code>format</code> names</h2>
      <p class="mb-6 max-w-3xl text-body-medium text-on-surface-variant">
        The seventeen names of Draft-07 §7.3, plus the three 1.x also published
        (<code>url</code>, <code>uuid</code>, <code>duration</code>). None is out of scope: the
        “unsupported” column of this table is empty. There is exactly one format table in the
        library and every entry points at the plugin that owns the grammar, so there is one regex
        per format and nothing to drift from it — 1.x shipped three tables and they disagreed with
        each other about <code>email</code>, <code>uuid</code> and <code>date-time</code>.
      </p>

      <div class="md-card md-card-outlined overflow-hidden">
        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="w-full min-w-[44rem] border-collapse text-body-medium">
            <thead>
              <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant text-label-large text-on-surface-variant">
                <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-medium">format</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-medium">Plugin subpath</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-medium">Method</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-medium">Note</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {
                formatRows.map((row) => (
                  <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/60 last:border-0">
                    <td class="whitespace-nowrap px-4 py-3 font-mono text-body-small text-on-surface">
                      {row.format}
                    </td>
                    <td class="whitespace-nowrap px-4 py-3 font-mono text-body-small text-on-surface-variant">
                      {row.plugin}
                    </td>
                    <td class="whitespace-nowrap px-4 py-3 font-mono text-body-small text-on-surface-variant">
                      {row.method}
                    </td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-3 text-body-small text-on-surface-variant">{row.note}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))
              }
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-body-medium text-on-surface-variant">
        A format name that is <em>not</em> in this table is an annotation and passes, which is what
        Draft-07 §7.2 requires. It is not an error. Adding the last four names
        (<code>idn-email</code>, <code>idn-hostname</code>, <code>uri-reference</code>,
        <code>regex</code>) is the entire difference between 86.54% and the current
        {conformance.percent}%: before them, a schema naming one threw at build time and produced no
        validator at all.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ================= Not supported ================= -->
    <section id="not-supported" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
      <h2 class="mb-2 text-headline-medium text-on-surface">What does not work</h2>
      <p class="mb-6 max-w-3xl text-body-medium text-on-surface-variant">
        The 101 failing cases, by cause. Every one is an entry in a typed skip list whose
        <code>cause</code> and <code>expiresWith</code> are closed string unions, so a failure
        cannot be recorded without naming what would fix it.
      </p>

      <div class="mb-6 md-card md-card-outlined overflow-hidden">
        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="w-full min-w-[46rem] border-collapse text-body-medium">
            <thead>
              <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant text-label-large text-on-surface-variant">
                <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-medium">Cause</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-3 text-right font-medium">Cases</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-medium">What it is</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-medium">Resolved by</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {
                failureCauses.map((row) => (
                  <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/60">
                    <td class="whitespace-nowrap px-4 py-3 align-top font-mono text-body-small text-on-surface">
                      {row.cause}
                    </td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-3 text-right align-top font-mono text-on-surface">
                      {row.count}
                    </td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-body-small text-on-surface-variant">
                      {row.what}
                    </td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top font-mono text-body-small text-on-surface-variant">
                      {row.resolvedBy}
                    </td>
                  </tr>
                ))
              }
              <tr>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-title-small text-on-surface">Total</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-right font-mono text-on-surface">101</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3" colspan="2"></td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="md-card md-card-outlined p-6">
          <h3 class="mb-3 text-title-medium text-on-surface">How they fail</h3>
          <ul class="space-y-2 text-body-medium">
            {
              failureModes.map((row) => (
                <li class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-4 border-b border-outline-variant/60 pb-2 last:border-0">
                  <span class="text-on-surface-variant">{row.mode}</span>
                  <span class="font-mono text-on-surface">{row.count}</span>
                </li>
              ))
            }
          </ul>
          <p class="mt-3 text-body-small text-on-surface-variant">
            An unsupported keyword is refused when the validator is built, not when it runs. A
            document Luq cannot honour never becomes a validator that quietly says yes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="md-card md-card-outlined p-6">
          <h3 class="mb-3 text-title-medium text-on-surface">
            The one you are most likely to meet
          </h3>
          <p class="mb-3 text-body-medium text-on-surface-variant">
            <code>null</code> at a field the document forbids it on. Absence is settled before any
            rule runs, so nullability is a presence policy rather than a rule. The converter writes
            <code>.optional()</code> for every non-required property, and a hand-written chain says
            it the same way.
          </p>
          <CodeBlock code={nullPresenceExample} showCopy={false} />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-6 md-card md-card-filled p-6">
        <h3 class="mb-3 text-title-medium text-on-surface">Limits worth knowing before you start</h3>
        <ul class="space-y-2 text-body-medium text-on-surface-variant">
          <li>
            <strong class="text-on-surface">No external or remote <code>$ref</code>.</strong>
            A loader that touches the network is not a default feature of a validation library.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-on-surface"><code>__proto__</code> is refused as a path segment.</strong>
            A deliberate decision, recorded as one, so that prototype pollution is unreachable.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-on-surface"><code>additionalProperties</code> does not consult
            <code>patternProperties</code>.</strong> It over-rejects in that combination.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-on-surface">A recursive <code>$ref</code> stops after the first
            iteration.</strong> No required suite case covers this, but a depth like
            <code>tree.child.name</code> is not validated.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-on-surface"><code>minLength</code> / <code>maxLength</code> count
            code units.</strong> A string of astral characters counts double.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

